#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:72
#: 78439498b4994526b54c7cbe31003423
msgid "Sometimes flow control signals are optionally provided in the connector. Some Telemetry radios utilize these signals:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:74
#: 6a1d08e6da774d55942201c8a28b3d37
msgid "CTS: Clear to Send Output to peripheral's labeled CTS input"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:75
#: 1ac876f5a08640119764fbb0492f345a
msgid "RTS: Request to Send Input from peripheral's labeled RTS output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:78
#: 226215c389a4481b8307eee1f8ed06bb
msgid "I2C"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:80
#: a69aa785f2a94f03b8670ece32cb05f5
msgid "I2C is used to connect certain sensors or peripherals. Most notably compasses (which are mandatory in many vehicles) and digital airspeed sensors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:85
#: 3d382545408a4b22ba8bac5375e3e6f3
msgid "SCL: Clock Output, connects to peripherals SCL pin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:86
#: 919635e456e14f72ab3396c518009834
msgid "SDA: Bi-directional Data, connects to peripherals SDA pin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:88
#: 54e86b4f114549c5a1829246e4b0e4f1
msgid "While I2C uses 3.3V signaling, often +5V is supplied in the connector along with the signals, since many I2C devices actually use this as main power. If not enough ports are provided, I2C expansion boards can allow more than one device to be connected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:90
#: 23e64c55a0b74940807d09d4f083f358
msgid "Some board-level autopilots require external 2K ohm pullups on the signal lines to 3.3V since they are not provided on the board, while some I2C peripherals provide the pull-ups. If the device does not operate properly, one should first be sure that the pull-ups are provided somewhere."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:93
#: 3fb8bb4dd93144de92ce9530e047e081
msgid "GPS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:95
#: 5c3aea7f57f04fadb2cc73807d550f20
msgid ":ref:`GPS<common-positioning-landing-page>` is usually attached to one of the UART ports, but some autopilots provide a connector dedicated to GPS and/or GPS/Compass which includes the I2C signals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:100
#: 4936a0bdfffe413da78c88f5c7397034
msgid "TX: Data Output, connected to the GPS RX input"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:101
#: 93630d279174442fa2a67284168802d9
msgid "RX: Data Input, connected to the GPS TX output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:103
#: 936ed9b0bb9741afad9a060b7909e7a8
msgid "Note the swapping of signals between autopilot and peripheral."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:105
#: d736aeb27d394146a0fce0a4a4043d51
msgid "usually +5V and GND are provided in the connector. If it is a GPS/Compass port, then the I2C signals will also be provided and attached to the same named signals on the compass sub-module of a GPS/Compass module."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:109
#: 1f656786ee724587a89cf4960a87f08e
msgid "PMU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:111
#: df7449603fd44d4a8bfb5b6cdae4d841
msgid "Most autopilots provide the means to attach to a Power Management Unit (PMU) of some kind. These :ref:`units<common-powermodule-landingpage>` provide any, or all, of the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:113
#: 499a97493a454404b70a6642c8d5a41b
msgid "A regulated +5V supply for the autopilot from the flight battery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:114
#: cd2eeee7f2bf466c8fe31a24e2978dd8
msgid "Monitoring of current from the flight battery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:115
#: c5ef1bce38244ba98db080e555feeb84
msgid "Monitoring of voltage from the flight battery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:120
#: 3e2bef18b699450ba606d7f260b4cce6
msgid "+5V: Regulated supply to autopilot"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:121
#: 284137968aa74a4fb06bdd42203f7e1e
msgid "CUR: Current Monitor output. Usually, a 0-3.3v analog voltage represents the current draw level"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:122
#: b31436a6de654153bc11381712a25919
msgid "VLT: Voltage Monitor output. Usually, a  0-3.3V analog voltage representing battery voltage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:123
#: e462fcece92244debb000e10299533cd
msgid "GND: Ground"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:125
#: d63204a21db44318af8e50ecad7ad2d4
msgid "Some \"smart\" battery/power monitors replace the CUR and VLT pins with I2C signals to provide digital information on battery status."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:127
#: fb9ada1ae7094751a1e747da2f9a613e
msgid "Many board-style autopilots fully integrate the PMU as part of the board with internal connections to the processor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:129
#: fa866554027145e0a3b4dee4b11199cd
msgid "In addition, many autopilots offer multiple PMU connections since ArduPilot firmware versions 4.0 and later support up to 10 PMUs/Battery Monitors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:132
#: 2fc0b978e095443db030ed4a3c33ba21
msgid "RCIN"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:134
#: f666212ef506425e89d3971e9c3312ab
msgid "Input from the radio control receiver is input on this pin. Most serial RC protocols (PPM, SBUS, DSM, etc.) are supported by ArduPilot and auto-detected. In addition, some autopilots provide dedicated connectors for DSM protocol satellite receivers which provide power to the receiver in addition to the input signal."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:136
#: bbd2f1877941487cae21eae2d920559c
msgid "As of firmware versions 4.0 and later, ArduPilot also allows an RC receiver to be attached to any UART port."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:141
#: 8e5da10c453843b0ae7160e85c7d953f
msgid "MAIN/AUX/OUT"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:143
#: 5b5990b80e254278a81fa5fda5a2509f
msgid "The primary outputs for controlling motors (via ESCs) and servos are provided by these pins/connectors. They are labeled either as MAIN/AUX outputs or just as OUTPUTs. These outputs provide the PWM or Dshot signals for motor ESC or servo control of flight surfaces. They can also be sometimes used as general purpose I/O pins for controlling relays, parachutes, grippers, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:145
#: f3aaa966ecca4327881f149fa7314860
msgid "Those controllers with MAIN/AUX output labels usually indicate that a IOMCU co-processor is being employed. These provide outputs intended for use as the motor/servo outputs and provide a redundant means of control via RC if the main autopilot fails. The MAIN outputs come from this co-processor, while the AUX designated outputs are controlled directly from the autopilot. Most board level autopilots do not use an IOMCU and have outputs only labeled OUTPUTx or Mx."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:147
#: 53bf1a05ede34104a5a314c02ec78882
msgid "This distinction is important since AUX outputs(and OUTPUTs from autopilots without an IOMCU) can be used as GPIOs as well as PWM or Dshot. While MAIN outputs can only be used for PWM, except for use as RELAY GPIOs. See :ref:`GPIOs <common-gpios>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:149
#: a8b7249afdec4a26b61915104beedee5
msgid "A few autopilots that do NOT use an IOMCU label their outputs as MAIN, so actually do have the capability of use as GPIOs and/or Dshot ESC control on these outputs. CUAV V5 Nano and Holybro Pixhawk 4 Mini are examples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:151
#: fe9439cf6ae14115aa3edebddf802fb3
msgid "Often these outputs are provided on 3-pin connector strips supplying or distributing servo power and ground, in addition to the individual output signals. This power is usually provided externally, such as by the ESC or a BEC, although some autopilots provide this power from internal regulators."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:154
#: 5ab0a06dc2a54f5aaabc324949f22297
msgid "CAN"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:156
#: f0f0f47bfe0b4fa08177e2652e1c121c
msgid ":ref:`CAN <common-canbus-setup-advanced>` bus is provided on many autopilots for use with :ref:`DroneCAN <common-uavcan-setup-advanced>` peripherals. ESCs, GPS/Compass, Rangefinders, and many other peripherals and sensors are being added to the list of available DroneCAN devices every day. CAN provides a robust method of communicating with peripherals with data integrity, even with long leads."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:162
#: 3905f9aa9dbd49a2a6f291d4c5d09496
msgid "CAN_H: CAN high side signal, connects to peripherals CAN_H pin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:163
#: 82bd956b69164f9d97a197e19ede20c5
msgid "CAN_L: CAN low side signal, connects to peripherals CAN_L pin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:165
#: 30862d2a537a4d028c115ca018a6b6c2
msgid "Power and ground are usually also provided on a standard 4-pin JST-GH connector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:168
#: d536f44adefe4c9f84426dea78b52f3d
msgid "SPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:170
#: 06514a6a0d45442592ede7dd756f9ab2
msgid "Most autopilots have processors with multiple SPI ports. While some ports are used for onboard peripherals, often one or more are provided externally for the addition of sensor chips such as barometers or IMUs, for redundancy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:175
#: 531e1346d62941c18f0891029e7a386b
msgid "CSx:  One or more chip selects for enabling the SPI peripheral"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:176
#: dbd99b2359b14408984e627474a866ae
msgid "MOSI: Master Out/Slave In Data, connects to the same signal on SPI peripheral"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:177
#: cbc2d1f2e3aa41f48a8a9eebbd5d2167
msgid "MISO: Master In/Slave Out Data, connects to the same signal on SPI peripheral"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:178
#: ab869b21aa944f5daed72976a1d5ef6f
msgid "SCK:  Clock Signal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:180
#: a61097999c5142a78978c58f66001601
msgid "SPI signal names can be labeled in many different ways. See the `SPI Wikipedia entry <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serial_Peripheral_Interface>`__ for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:183
#: 4ee69bb8b860457d9857150b7660350e
msgid "SAFETY SW/LED"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:185
#: 17afe88bd8a94a2197bd659c85da5a93
msgid "Many autopilots provide dedicated GPIOs on a connector for adding the optional safety switch and notification LEDs that ArduPilot supports. Usually, these are offered on autopilots that utilize an IOMCU co-processor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:190
#: af5e67273eab4e20be9622c5ab946e1e
msgid "+3.3V :  Supply to the LED and Switch"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:191
#: e404cd73d96947a8b1eb6ede37b842b5
msgid "LED:     Drives the ground side of the notification LED"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:192
#: 6741640aef3244b395a32acfcd9f6d5e
msgid "SW:      Senses if +3.3V is present to indicate switch closure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:196
#: 5b70aa6d72ea41b7aa3952f8295c09fe
msgid "BUZZER"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:198
#: 9846c755b1a747be8acd9d5016b92547
msgid "A -BUZZ output is sometimes provided for a passive or active buzzer for system notification sounds, and provides a switched ground connection to the buzzer. See :ref:`Buzzer<common-buzzer>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:201
#: 185b903ac0f64d3381587202852a7e2a
msgid "ANALOG INPUTS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-flight-controller-io.rst:203
#: 58df5a87dc744cb683210203f31781aa
msgid "Often analog voltage measurement pins are provided. These are used for current and/or voltage sensing from a power monitor (if a dedicated connector has not been provided), other system voltage monitor points, or for analog :ref:`RSSI<common-rssi-received-signal-strength-indication>` input."
msgstr ""
